\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{quadratic-forms}

\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Full rational diagonals are discrete under lattice containment}

\p{Let #{Q} be nondegenerate on a finite-dimensional rational space
#{V}, choose a \vocabk{compatible compact-open reference tuple}{fcap-004C}
#{U}, and fix a rational basis #{b}. Let #{L_p} be its
#{\mathbb Z_p}-span and #{H_p=\{g\in O_p:gL_p=L_p\}} as in
\ref{fcap-004D}. Assume the \em{eventual lattice containment}

##{U_p^O\subseteq H_p\quad\text{for all but finitely many primes }p.}

Then each \vocabk{rational diagonal subgroup}{fcap-004K}

##{\delta_{\mathbb A}^O(O(Q)),\qquad
\delta_{\mathbb A}^{SO}(SO(Q)),\qquad
\delta_{\mathbb A}^{\Spin}(\Spin(Q))}

is discrete in its full adelic group. In particular this holds for
the integral tuple of \ref{fcap-004D}, its rational-basis changes
and its finite shrinkings from \ref{fcap-004E}.}

\subtree{
\taxon{Proof}
\p{Put #{W_p=U_p^O\cap H_p}. The groups #{W_p} are open, since
both factors are open by \ref{fcap-004C} and \ref{fcap-004D}.
Choose a finite set #{F} outside which #{U_p^O\subseteq H_p};
then #{W_p=U_p^O} for #{p\notin F}. Thus

##{K_W=\prod_p W_p\subseteq O_f(U)}

is an open identity neighborhood. Indeed, inside the open principal
stage #{O_F(U)} it imposes an open condition on only the finitely
many coordinates in #{F}, and leaves the other reference factors
unchanged. This uses the stage topology of \ref{fcap-004F}.}

\p{Let #{M_b(g_\infty)} be the real matrix of #{g_\infty\in O_\infty}
in the extended basis, and put

##{A=\left\{g_\infty\in O_\infty:
\left|M_b(g_\infty)_{ij}-\delta_{ij}\right|<\tfrac12
\text{ for every }i,j\right\}.}

Here #{\delta_{ij}} is the Kronecker delta. The finitely many matrix
entries are continuous by \ref{fcap-0044}, so #{A} is open and
contains the identity. Hence #{A\times K_W} is an open identity
neighborhood in #{O_{\mathbb A}(U)}.}

\p{Suppose a rational #{g\in O(Q)} has diagonal in this
neighborhood. Every #{g_p} stabilizes #{L_p}; therefore each entry
of its rational matrix #{M_b(g)} belongs to #{\mathbb Z_p} at
every prime. A reduced fraction #{a/d}, with #{d>0}, has this
property only if #{d=1}: a prime divisor #{p} of #{d} does not
divide #{a}, giving negative #{p}-adic valuation. Thus all the
entries of #{M_b(g)} are integers. Their real values differ from
#{\delta_{ij}} by less than #{1/2}; an integer with that property
must equal #{\delta_{ij}}. Consequently #{g=1}. This isolates the
identity in the orthogonal diagonal image. Translation by each
diagonal element isolates every point of that image.}

\p{Pull the neighborhood back under the continuous inclusion
#{SO_{\mathbb A}(U)\to O_{\mathbb A}(U)} from \ref{fcap-004J}.
Naturality from \ref{fcap-004K} shows that its intersection with
the rational SO image contains only the identity: the underlying
rational orthogonal transformation is already the identity.
This proves SO discreteness.}

\p{Pull this SO neighborhood back along the continuous map
#{\rho_{\mathbb A}:\Spin_{\mathbb A}(U)\to SO_{\mathbb A}(U)}.
A rational Spin point in this preimage has rational projection
#{1}, by naturality and injectivity of the SO diagonal. If
#{\dim V>0}, the field version of the scalar-kernel statement in
\ref{fcap-001C} gives precisely #{s=1} or #{s=-1}.
The real Spin group is Hausdorff, and the scalar elements #{1}
and #{-1} are distinct there. Choose an open real identity
neighborhood #{B_\infty} excluding #{-1}. Intersect the preceding
preimage with #{B_\infty\times\Spin_f(U)}. The intersection is
open and its rational diagonal points consist of #{1} alone.
Translation proves Spin discreteness.}

\p{If #{\dim V=0}, the actual vector-generated Spin carrier is
#{\{1\}}, as in \ref{fcap-003H}; both orthogonal carriers are
also singleton. The conclusion is immediate. In particular there
is no extra scalar #{-1} in this generated zero-dimensional carrier.}

\p{For the integral tuple, #{U_p^O=H_p} at every prime.
Finite shrinking only replaces reference subgroups by smaller ones,
so it preserves containment. Changing rational basis changes
#{H_p} at only finitely many primes by \ref{fcap-004E}.
Adjoin these primes to the exceptional set to retain the hypothesis.}
}

\p{The separation has two independent parts: finite-place integrality
forces rational matrix entries to be integers, and the real bound
then forces the identity matrix. The finite projection discards the
second condition. With the rational groups given the discrete topology,
injectivity and the proved discrete images make these full diagonals
topological embeddings; this argument asserts no such property for
the finite diagonals.}

\p{\citet{Example 2.3, p. 3}{conrad2011adelic} proves discreteness
for rational points in classical affine full adelic points. The proof
above supplies the chosen-tuple version under its explicit containment
hypothesis, using \ref{fcap-001C} for the Spin scalar kernel.
The exact O and SO criteria are
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens.discreteTopology_range_fullAdelicOrthogonalDiagonal_of_eventually_integral}
and
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens.discreteTopology_range_fullAdelicSpecialOrthogonalDiagonal_of_eventually_integral};
these two formal statements even allow degenerate forms.
TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens.discreteTopology_range_fullAdelicSpinDiagonal}
proves the Spin conclusion for every nondegenerate compatible tuple,
using a separate eventual-containment theorem. The explicit containment
assumption is retained here to expose the neighborhood argument.}
